      "rationale": "The estimating verb is present only as wording. Stems say Estimate how many unit tiles cover the rectangle in all and About how many tiles in total will you use, but the key is the exact product and the distractors sit adjacent to it, so an approximate answer is marked wrong. The task performed is exact array counting wearing estimation language."
    },
    "comprehensive_coverage": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "Squares and rectangles are both tiled, the arrays cover shapes from 2 by 2 up to 10 by 10 including non-square rectangles in both row-heavy and column-heavy orientations, and a metre-tile garden situation shows the same idea outside the bare array."
    },
    "cognitive_capacity": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "Dimensions stay at ten or below so every tile count is a basic multiplication fact or a repeated addition a Grade 3 Quarter 1 pupil can carry out, and phrases like covered edge-to-edge give the tiling condition in plain language."
    },
    "variant_comprehensiveness": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "Three presentations occur: a described array, a displayed array introduced by Look at the 4x3 array, and a real situation where Pia tiles a garden with one-metre squares. Multiple choice and cloze both appear."
    },
